<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lê Long Đĩnh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">986–1009</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Le Long Dinh, Lê Ngọa Triều</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lê Long Đĩnh was the last ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-le-dynasty">Early Lê dynasty</a>, reigning from 1005 to 1009 after killing his brother in the succession struggle that followed the death of their father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Đại Hành</a>. Vietnamese chronicles remember him as cruel and so ill that he held audience lying down, hence the name Lê Ngọa Triều. On his death the court chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to">Lý Công Uẩn</a>, founder of the Lý dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/le-long-dinh.jpg" alt="Lê Long Đĩnh" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kien1980v at Vietnamese Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lelongdinh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
